rand masters in  this photo  but my opponent was a guy right here i'm  trying to use my cursor but it's not  capturing my cursor oh there we go  okay so i'm playing ryan murphy  who  has been in very close contact with  ikaru and fabiano and  a few other grand masters  and  yeah i did a little bit of preparation  for the game  and i found his old chess.com account  and i knew to expect a kerocon which did  happen after e4 and c6  and it took me a little bit of time to  decide what to play because i could have  gone for london or english already but i  figured i would test him in his main  opening  and i went for a sideline pawn d3  which i've played a few times before i  played against sagar shah in the  speedchest championship match also  played a maybe once or twice against  levy  in the same event  and d3 kind of signifies that white's  going for a king's indian attack salap  with knight d2 g3 bishop g2 knight f3  and this is basically what happened  after d5 knight d2  ryan chose a pretty solid line g6  and i go for  yeah basic king's indian attack g3 25  bishop g2 97 castling  castling we both played the moves pretty  quickly in the opening  and this isn't the most confrontational  opening but i decided to go for this  because  it's a position that just has a lot of  life and it's not something that  liquidates too quickly  and i actually had this position  on my computer screen about half hour  before the round began  and i prepared the move pawn a4  now the main line here is rook to e1  which  is an attempt to kind of pressure the  e-file threatening to take and then win  upon  but after pawn d4  black scores pretty well here  and pawn a4 is um a bit more flexible of  a move uh because there's cases where i  want to keep my rook on f1 in the event  that black plays d4 i can go for knight  c4 and then later pawn f4 ideas  so  after i played a4 ryan took kind of the  first think of the game and ultimately  decided to play a5  which is a very flexible move and i  played c3 still trying to stay flexible  and here the the main move for black is  to play knight a6  which intends to go for knight c5  and if black went for this then i'd  probably just place all the queen c2 uh  then decide whether to put the rook on  e1 or d1  but instead in the game uh ryan played  pawn h6  which was kind of a waiting move um i  think in hindsight this move was a  little bit slow  even though the intention is to prevent  knight g5 and to prepare bishop d6 we're  gonna see that white gets some  somewhat quick initiative  after rookie one i am threatening to  take on d5 and win the e pawn so black  has to do something about it  uh played rook e8  and now i keep initiative i take on d5  take  and knight c4  so now i have three attackers against  the pawn  and black is put into  some kind of decision moment  how to defend the pawn and there are a  few moves here  um now black doesn't really want to play  f6 this is just  being really mean to the bishop on g7  after f6 i would have d4 which would be  quite nice for white  uh during the game i was expecting  bishop g4 which i think was a better  choice uh just  pinning the knight and if h3 that can  trade  uh and if queen takes there's knight d7  and um i was expecting to go into  something like this now maybe not knight  g7 right away because i do have knight  d6 here  but i'll move like queen c7  and um the game would go on  uh instead  black played knight d7 here  and uh as we're gonna see the  bishop just got stuck on c8 and i was  still happy to go for the move d4  and because white has a slight lean  development  it makes sense for me to try and open  the position so after it takes we traded  rooks takes and i recapture on d4  and uh here the pawn structure is almost  completely symmetrical  if i had a pawn on h3 it would be  exactly symmetrical but there are a few  kind of imbalances  my knights are a little bit more active  and because the queen is on e8 i do have  ideas of knight to d6 to try and uh get  the bishop hair  and yeah here ryan took a bit of a think  and decided to play knight f6  um during the game i was thinking uh  like knight e5 or knight c5 or even  knight b6 these are all possible moves  but after knight f6 i  realized that i can just kind of calmly  complete development  now it's not easy to find a good square  for my bishop because the knight's  controlling  e3 and f4 and i can't develop to g5  obviously  but i played the move bishop d2  and  it was actually pretty simple uh plan  for white is to go for queen d3  and then rook to e1 to control the  e-file  now i should note i can't play queen b3  right away because after queen  to e1  i'd be in some trouble bishop f1 is met  with bishop to h3  so bishop d2 was a precise move order  and uh yeah we reached a position where  it just wasn't easy to find a clear plan  for black as if black tries to play a  similar move  i can start targeting the undefended b7  pawn with a move like queen b3  and then having ideas of knight e6 to  follow  along with rookie one and black is  clearly under a lot of pressure here  so instead uh ryan played a bit  creatively and went for the move between  f8  getting off the e file defending the  pawn another time  after i play queen b3  pawn h5 was played  and now it's clear that black has ideas  of either playing pawn h4  or bishop to h6  and  at first during the game i thought this  was a creative idea to uh maybe try and  trade a little bit try and weaken my  king side in some way  but the more i thought here the more i  realized i can kind of just ignore  what black is trying to do  and carry through with my own ideas  and i started with the move rookie one  just controlling the open e file  and after pawn h4 i just continued to  ignore black's play on the king side and  play the move knight to e5  uh just improving the knight  and now the development for white is  just really nice the knights are well  defended in the center  a bishop is pressuring d5 along with the  queen  and the queen is a line with the king so  there's some x-ray vision  against the king on g8 for example if  the knight ever moves if we play just a  dumb move knight c7 and then i'd have  knight takes g6 because of the pin on f  pawn  so in some sense the knight is pinned  along the diagonal  and we're going to see that black gets a  little bit paralyzed in in just a few  moves  now bishop h6 was played which is an  interesting attempt to trade bishops  and if i knew that black was going to go  for a bishop trade a few moves earlier  i would have been happy to basically  trade off my worst place piece in the  position the bishop on d2 but then i  realized here that  this bishop isn't so useful for black  either  and the general rule is that when you're  controlling more space and more center  you should avoid trains and minor pieces  just because you want to put the  opponent in a more crown position  and i decided here to play the move pawn  f4  just avoiding uh trades entirely  and also reinforcing the knight  and even though pawn f4 maybe looks a  little bit weakening at first uh moving  upon from my king there's no real way  for black to exploit the king in this  position and this kind of induced black  to go for the move queen c5  which looks active at first pinning my  knight  but it just invited the move bishop to  e3 which i was very happy to play  aligning with the queen  and now the queen is just regretting its  life decisions  and i should point out here the knight  on d5 is pinned  to the pawn on f7 so black has no time  to take on e3 because this is just  getting mated knight takes g6 would be  checkmate  so  what black had to do here is just  retreat the queen  all the way back to f8  now there was a funny line i was  thinking of during the game  it looks like black can try the move  knight g4  just to try and muddy the waters have  two attackers against the bishop  but this would walk into a really really  nice tactic  knight to e6  attacking the queen twice so basically  double check against the queen  now this move also obstructs a bishop  from defending the knight on g4  and this is relevant because after a  move like queen to d6 i can happily take  the knight  after it takes  take the bishop on h6 and white is just  up a piece at the end of this line  and  when i was calculating these variations  during the game  i was just getting really excited  thinking that  my experience from a few days ago  of riding the icelandic horse  would be very nice to include  in in the thumbnail photo because the  nylon e6  is a very majestic creature almost as  majestic as an icelandic horse  but sadly this didn't happen in the game  after bishop e3 queen f8 was played  and i decided to reposition the bishop  on f2 where black can't trade it off  and now the bishop is just really nicely  placed keeps my king safe  and after pawn h3 bishop f3 the bishop  now restricts any piece from coming to  g4 so there's no knight g4 there's no  bishop g4  and i was thinking during the game that  black is just really close to being  completely paralyzed it's almost  zugswang in this position  um very difficult to find a move for  black just because knight is still  essentially pinned along the diagonal if  knight ever moves there's still knight  takes g6  the bishop is completely locked out  this knight is tied down to defending  the nylon d5 the bishop is tied down to  defending the pawn in b7  and if the rook tries to move to b8 to  over defend the pawn then i have the  move knight takes c6  and i'm exploiting the pin on the b file  so  what ended up happening was  queen d6 was played  which looks like a logical  somewhat active move  but the problem with this move  is it walks into some tactics because it  leaves f7 undefended  and i have the very explosive move here  on c4  and it turns out that black is losing  material by force  and um  to my surprise  ryan actually resigned in this position  just didn't play on i should also note  that he had uh less than a minute left  when he did resign i was getting into  some very serious time trouble  um this is move 24 so it's still  somewhat early in the game  but to explain why c4 is just so deadly  is if the knight moves there's actually  probably a few tactics at win for white  but i was planning knight takes f7  exploiting the fact that after king  takes f7 there's pawn c5  with the discovered check and uh white  is just winning the queen here  and  uh basically wherever the knight moves  i'm gonna have the same tactic like if  knight c7  again i can take on f7 and i haven't  checked this game yet with engine i've  actually been doing this whole recap  with engine turned off  so i'm just gonna verify real quick  that yeah after pawn c4 this is  completely winning for white  and  all the top engine moves are uh are not  moving the knight like bishop g7 stop  moving  yeah any case white's winning  a lot of material and  resigning i guess was justified for  black  so overall i was pretty happy with the  game um i think it was a pretty clean  game on my part  and  afterwards i wasn't entirely sure  what the crucial mistake was from  black's perspective  it may have been  earlier in um  in the middle game  perhaps allowing  this line um  allowing takes in knight c4 maybe black  is already under a little bit of  pressure  uh i think during during the game i  thought black should really get the  knight to c5 as quickly as possible  but um yeah overall i  have no complaints about this game this  nice positional build up and i hope  there's a thing or two that viewers can  learn  from this game  and it was from an opening that i don't  really play too often it was a king's  indian attack against the cairo khan  but maybe after this game i'll have to  play this this whole thing more often if  it works as well  so uh that just about does it  if you want to follow the broadcast of  the tournament i will leave links in the  description uh t